The question is: "Can an interface declare static methods?" in PHP.

In PHP, interfaces can declare static methods. Since PHP 5.1, interfaces can declare static methods. The implementing class must implement them as static methods.

For example:
```php
interface Foo {
    public static function bar();
}

class Baz implements Foo {
    public static function bar() {
        return "hello";
    }
}
```

This is valid PHP. So the answer is "да".

Let me think about PHP's try-catch-finally behavior.

In PHP, if an exception is thrown inside a `finally` block, it will replace the original exception or return value from the `try` block. The `finally` block executes after `try`/`catch`, and if it throws a new exception, that new exception propagates instead of the original one.

For example:
```php
try {
    return "try";
} finally {
    throw new Exception("finally");
}
```
This will throw the exception from finally, not return "try".

So the statement "Исключение, выброшенное внутри блока finally, никак не влияет на результат try-блока" is FALSE. The exception in finally DOES affect the result — it replaces it.

The question asks what will be printed by this PHP code:

```php
function inc(&$x) { $x++; }
$n = 5;
inc($n);
echo $n;
```

The function `inc` takes a parameter by reference (`&$x`). So when `$n = 5` is passed to `inc($n)`, the parameter `$x` is a reference to `$n`. Inside the function, `$x++` increments the value of `$n` directly. So after `inc($n)`, `$n` becomes 6. Then `echo $n` prints 6.

The question is about whether property hooks (get/set directly in property declaration) were introduced in PHP 8.4.

Property hooks were indeed introduced in PHP 8.4. They allow defining get and set logic directly in the property declaration, like:

```php
class Example {
    public string $name {
        get => $this->name;
        set(string $value) { $this->name = strtoupper($value); }
    }
}
```

This was one of the major features of PHP 8.4, released in November 2024.

